  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "If the point \\((x,-4)\\) lies on the straight line joining the points \\((0,8)\\) and \\((-4,0)\\) in the \\(xy\\)-plane, then \\(x\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-2\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(-8\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\)",
    "E": "\\(-6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "An equation for the line will let us substitute the known coordinate \\(y=-4\\). The two given points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, so the line is not vertical and can be written as \\(y=mx+h\\).\n\nThe point \\((0,8)\\) gives \\(h=8\\). Using \\((-4,0)\\) then gives\n\\[\n0=-4m+8,\n\\qquad m=2.\n\\]\nThus the line is \\(y=2x+8\\). Since \\((x,-4)\\) lies on it,\n\\[\n-4=2x+8,\n\\qquad 2x=-12,\n\\qquad x=-6.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(\\boxed{x=-6}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
